<section id="introduction"><h2>2 Introduction</h2>
<p>Preliminary tug calculations are provided for bollard pull, escort force, girting stability, fender contact and energy, towline loads, and energy and emissions. The energy workflow is evaluated here because its public example can be checked directly by an energy balance without adopting unsourced empirical coefficients.</p>
<p>The example is a software regression case, not measured operating data. No named craft, equipment selection, drawings or field measurements are represented. Entry points and evidence locators are given in <a href="#locators">the source register</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="controls"><h2>3 Duty-cycle power balance</h2>
<p>The power balance is evaluated against an assumed engine baseline. Demand above this baseline is assigned to battery discharge; the baseline is a dispatch choice, not a certified machinery limit.</p>
<figure>$chart<figcaption>Figure A. Public example: propulsion demand and battery discharge by phase. The dashed line is the engine baseline. Hover over or focus a bar to inspect its values.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Blue denotes propulsion demand. Amber denotes battery discharge. The dashed red line denotes the assumed engine baseline; it does not denote a failed equipment rating.</p>
<p id="chart-detail" aria-live="polite">Select a bar to inspect the phase and component.</p>
<p>Demand above the engine baseline is supplied by the ideal battery model. The component power rating is required before a demand-to-capacity comparison can be made.</p>
</section>

<section id="basis"><h2>4 Design data and assessment basis</h2>
<p>The public example supplies installed propulsion power of $power kW and an engine baseline fraction of $baseline. These are illustrative inputs, marked ASSUMED - to be confirmed for any physical application.</p>
<p>The duty cycle contains transit, assist and standby phases. The complete phase inputs are recorded in Table A. The engine baseline is deliberately below the peak demand to demonstrate battery discharge during assist.</p>
<h3>4.1 Criteria and rationale</h3>
<p>The calculation criterion is agreement with the piecewise constant energy balance: energy is power integrated over time. The reference is <a href="#source-theory">the public power definition</a>. Agreement tests arithmetic, not equipment suitability. A physical acceptance criterion requires a battery discharge rating and usable capacity after reserve, loss and ageing allowances; those data are absent.</p>
