<section id="method"><h2>5 Methodology and equations</h2>
<p>Phase demand is calculated from installed power and the phase power fraction. Phase energy is calculated by integrating that constant demand over its duration, following <a href="#source-theory">the power definition</a>.</p>
<div class="eq">P<sub>j</sub> = P<sub>installed</sub> f<sub>j</sub>; E<sub>cycle</sub> = Σ P<sub>j</sub> Δt<sub>j</sub>.</div>
<p>P denotes power in kW; j identifies a phase; f is the dimensionless phase power fraction; Δt is phase duration in h; E is energy in kWh; Σ denotes summation over the phases. The installed-power relation defines the input fraction, rather than introducing an empirical coefficient.</p>
<div class="eq">P<sub>base</sub> = b P<sub>installed</sub>; P<sub>battery,j</sub> = max(P<sub>j</sub> − P<sub>base</sub>, 0); E<sub>battery</sub> = Σ P<sub>battery,j</sub> Δt<sub>j</sub>.</div>
<p>b is the assumed dimensionless engine baseline fraction. The positive difference assigns only demand above the baseline to battery discharge. This dispatch assumption is combined with the same energy integral; it is not a class rule. Peak demand and peak battery power are the maximum phase values. Battery energy share is discharge energy divided by cycle energy. The equations are implemented by the <a href="#source-api">documented energy functions</a>.</p>
<p>The model does not credit charging between discharge phases. Energy above the baseline is accumulated without a time-dependent state of charge. The output therefore represents total ideal discharge over the selected cycle, not necessarily the minimum battery capacity with managed recharge.</p>
</section>

<section id="results"><h2>6 Worked example and results</h2>
<p>The public regression example is evaluated by calling the module with the shared illustrative duty-cycle inputs. Each phase is also evaluated separately to obtain the plotted power demand and battery contribution.</p>
<p>The cycle requires $total_energy_kwh kWh, with peak demand of $peak_power_kw kW. At the assumed engine baseline of $baseline_engine_kw kW, peak battery discharge is $battery_power_kw kW and discharge energy is $battery_energy_kwh kWh. All returned sizing quantities are recorded in Table B.</p>
<p>Assist governs peak demand and battery discharge. Transit and standby do not exceed the chosen baseline; that condition establishes neither available charging power nor a recharge schedule. Battery share is reported as a fraction in Table B, not as a fuel-saving percentage.</p>
</section>

<section id="validation"><h2>7 Validation evidence</h2>
<p>Existing regression checks are inspected as software evidence. The tug test file contains $test_count test definitions distributed across the calculation areas in Table C; this inventory is not a count of field trials or passed tests.</p>
<p>The energy tests contain literal expected cycle energy and peak-shaving results. Other checks exercise propulsion dependence, force scaling, stability boundaries, fender overlap and energy, towline sizing, and emissions. No measured battery discharge record or separately published hybrid benchmark is identified in the inspected tug source, tests and documentation.</p>
<p>The report checks compare all sizing fields with fresh module calls, compare displayed values at their printed precision, and include hand-calculated energy, peak power, baseline and battery-share anchors. A separate case uses an assist duration of 2.5 h to check the duration factor in discharge energy. Numerical tolerance is applied to arithmetic comparisons. Field validation remains NOT_EVALUATED; confirmation requires measured power and time histories at a declared electrical or mechanical boundary.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits"><h2>8 Findings, limits and recommendations</h2>
<p>The calculation establishes an ideal discharge demand for the assumed profile. Installed battery capacity remains not evaluated because its governing data are absent.</p>
<h3>8.1 Recommendations</h3>
<p>A measured phase profile at the selected power boundary is recommended before equipment sizing, because energy depends directly on phase power and duration under the <a href="#source-theory">energy integral</a>. A time-dependent charge and discharge assessment with efficiency, usable capacity, reserve and ageing inputs is recommended before selecting a battery, because those terms are absent from the <a href="#source-api">sizing calculation</a>.</p>
<p>Input validation is recommended at the application boundary. The underlying energy function checks installed power and an empty profile, but does not reject negative phase durations, out-of-range phase fractions or all non-finite inputs. This report checks the selected profile before calculation. The module is not modified by this assessment.</p>
<h3>8.2 Not covered</h3>
<p>Bollard pull and escort estimates, girting stability, fender optimization and berthing energy, towline strength and recoil, and fuel and emissions are not evaluated numerically here. Their implementations include empirical coefficients or simplified criteria whose cited source editions and clauses are incomplete in the inspected code. Those values require a separate basis review before engineering use. No nominal class factor is adopted in this example.</p>
</section>

